{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790361401,"nonce":"5M06sTBka/h9g8BT+w5qOMcvzQk=","title":"Async handler operations compile in shapes the runtime cannot drive","message":"Two shapes compile without a diagnostic and misbehave. Found by reading crates/zyntax_embed/src/krio_lowering.rs, crates/passes/krio_adapter/src/abi_emit.rs and crates/compiler/src/cranelift_backend.rs; not reproduced.\n\n1. An `async def` handler operation with no `Resume\u003cT\u003e` parameter. Only resumable operations enter `handler_resolution`, so the perform stays a plain `PerformEffect` and the Cranelift arm calls the operation's promise entry directly: the perform's value is the `*Promise` pointer.\n2. An `async def` resumable operation performed from a function that is not `async`. `apply_krio_effect_lowering` gives the performer a sync entry (`generate_sync_entry`) that loops on its poll function until it returns non-zero. When the handler awaits, `__zyntax_effect_launch_handler` parks it and returns 0; the perform site returns that 0 without advancing the state slot, so the entry re-polls the performer from the same state, performs again and launches another handler, and nothing drives the timer the first handler parked on.\n\nDone: both shapes are refused at compile time with a diagnostic naming the handler operation and the performer, or each gets a lowering that works (an async operation without `Resume` awaited at the perform site of an async performer; a sync performer that drives its handler's timers).","files":null}]}